<p>Sedation dentistry — sometimes called <strong>"sleep dentistry"</strong> — uses medication to keep you calm and comfortable during treatment. Depending on the option, you'll feel anywhere from mildly relaxed to deeply at ease, so a visit you've been dreading becomes something you can get through with no stress.</p>

<h2>Our sedation options</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li><strong>Nitrous oxide ("laughing gas").</strong> Inhaled, mild, takes effect within minutes and wears off in minutes. You can drive yourself home.</li>
  <li><strong>Oral conscious sedation.</strong> A prescription pill taken before your appointment. You'll feel deeply relaxed and a little drowsy. Needs a ride home.</li>
  <li><strong>IV sedation.</strong> Medication delivered through an IV by a trained provider. Deeper relaxation, typically used for longer or more complex visits like full-arch implant surgery. Needs a ride home.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When sedation makes sense</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Significant dental anxiety or a difficult dental history</li>
  <li>Long appointments combining several procedures</li>
  <li>Full-arch implant cases (<a href="/services/all-on-4/">All-on-4 / All-on-X</a>)</li>
  <li>Complex extractions or grafting</li>
  <li>A strong gag reflex</li>
</ul>

<h2>Safety</h2>
<p>Every sedation patient is monitored throughout the visit. We review your medical history, current medications, and any concerns before we begin. If sedation isn't appropriate for you medically, we'll tell you and suggest alternatives.</p>
